{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Current treatments have very low efficacy and can only alleviate some of its symptoms. Medication therapy is the mainstay of current treatment methods. Not only has this treatment had very little effect on motor recovery (2), but also the unwanted side effects that occur with continued use of medications are a major barrier to their use (3). Current efforts in improving the recovery of central nervous system (CNS) focus on two aspects: 1) stimulation of neurogenesis or regeneration and 2) neuroprotection or prevention of secondary damage (4, 5). However, there is still significant disagreement over the new treatment strategies. For instance, older studies had demonstrated that inflammation caused by autoimmune response leads to exacerbation of SCI and motor impairment (6, 7). Nonetheless, recent animal research showed that the postinjury autoimmune reactions provoke beneficial endogenous responses following SCI (8). ... Although several studies that evaluate the effectiveness of active and passive immunization in SCI are available, conflicting findings have been reported between studies.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge (limited efficacy of treatments, focus areas) and highlights important uncertainties and conflicting findings about immunization approaches in SCI."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "On the other hand, it is not yet clear which active and inactive immunogenic methods are more effective in improving the motor function of animals. Accordingly, there is still no conclusion on the role of passive immunization with immune cells activated by MBP or on active immunization with injection of MBP and its derivatives in the treatment of SCI in the literature. Hence, the present study aims to perform a systematic review and meta-analysis to determine the effectiveness of passive or active immunization with MBP on motor recovery in animal models following SCI.",
      "reason": "The authors state the gap in knowledge and explicitly explain the need and objective for performing the systematic review and meta-analysis."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No text in the provided evidence discusses existing similar systematic reviews, nor cites or explains the need relative to previous reviews."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "These studies indicate that the presence of immune T cells in the injury site increases secretion of nervous growth factors, improves the tissue environment surrounding the damaged neurons, protects the remaining myelin, and eventually, leads to enhancement of motor recovery (4). It has also been demonstrated that proper regulation of immune response following SCI may have an essential role in axonal regeneration, prevention of secondary injuries, and SCI recovery. In addition, local transplantation of macrophages was associated with some degree of motor recovery in the literature (9, 10). Myelin Basic Protein (MBP) is a surface antigen expressed on cells in the CNS. Available studies show that transplantation of activated immune cells against this antigen is associated with improved motor recovery in animals with SCI (11, 12). Thus, transplantation of cells activated by this antigen, may selectively affect the CNS and reduce nonspecific inflammatory responses in other tissues (13). On the other hand, administration of this protein or similar peptides, as an active immunogenic process, could have significant effects on improving motor function following SCI (14-16).",
      "reason": "The introduction provides a brief proposed mechanism: immune T cells and MBP-targeted responses may increase neurotrophic factors, improve tissue environment, protect myelin and thereby enhance motor recovery."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No logic model or diagram showing hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes is presented in the provided evidence."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "PICO was defined as follows: Problem (P) included animals in which SCI injury was induced. Intervention (I) was the injection of MBP-activated T cells (passive immunization) or administration of MBP or MBP-modified peptides, inducing intrinsic immunity to MBP (active immunization). Comparison (C) consisted of a comparison with control animals, which were induced with SCI but had not received treatment, and outcome (O) was an assessment of motor function by BBB testing.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly states its objective/question using a question-formulation framework (PICO), defining population, interventions, comparator, and outcome."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "PICO was defined as follows: Problem (P) included animals in which SCI injury was induced. Intervention (I) was the injection of MBP-activated T cells (passive immunization) or administration of MBP or MBP-modified peptides, inducing intrinsic immunity to MBP (active immunization). Comparison (C) consisted of a comparison with control animals, which were induced with SCI but had not received treatment, and outcome (O) was an assessment of motor function by BBB testing.",
      "reason": "For an intervention review, the planned comparisons are stated explicitly using PICO, specifying interventions (active/passive immunization) and comparison (control animals without treatment)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria in the present study consisted of controlled studies that had evaluated the efficacy of immune cells activated against MBP protein in motor function improvementafter SCI. Controlled studies were defined as studies that had a control group without any treatment (placebo group or vehicle group), in addition to the group treated with active/passive immunization with MBP . ... Irrelevant, duplicate and review studies were excluded. Moreover, lack of motor function evaluation and combination therapy were the other exclusion criteria in the current meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "Study characteristics and eligibility are specified: population (SCI animals), interventions (active/passive MBP immunization), comparison (control group without treatment), outcomes (motor function), and design restrictions (controlled studies). Exclusion criteria (duplicates, reviews, lack of motor function evaluation, combination therapy) are also listed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "An extensive search was conducted on Medline, Embase, Scopus, and Web of Science databases until the end of 2020. ... Although only animal studies were included in the current systematic review, animal study filter was not applied in the search, in order to prevent the loss of relevant records. A manual search was also performed ... To search for Grey literature, the thesis section was searched in the ProQuest database. In addition, Google and Google Scholar search engines were searched to find additional resources. Finally, authors of the relevant studies were contacted to gain access to their unpublished or pre-print data.",
      "reason": "Report characteristics are specified: time limit (search until end of 2020), language policy noted in text_5 ('no language restrictions were imposed'), and efforts to include unpublished/grey literature and contact authors indicate consideration of publication/report status."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Moreover, lack of motor function evaluation and combination therapy were the other exclusion criteria in the current meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly excludes studies that lacked measurement of the motor outcome (i.e., not measured). The manuscript also states that when information was not reported authors were contacted (text_6), but it does not explicitly state whether studies were subsequently excluded specifically because outcome results were not reported; therefore the distinction is only partially addressed.",
      "confidence": 85
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "PICO was defined as follows: Problem (P) included animals in which SCI injury was induced. Intervention (I) was the injection of MBP-activated T cells (passive immunization) or administration of MBP or MBP-modified peptides, inducing intrinsic immunity to MBP (active immunization). Comparison (C) consisted of a comparison with control animals, which were induced with SCI but had not received treatment, and outcome (O) was an assessment of motor function by BBB testing.",
      "reason": "Groups used in syntheses are specified and linked to the review comparisons: active versus passive MBP immunization compared to untreated SCI control animals, with motor (BBB) outcome defined and used in analyses."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "Considering that motor function recovery following SCI needs a 4-week time window after the injury in a rat model, studies with a follow-up period of less than four weeks were excluded.",
      "reason": "A rationale is provided for the notable eligibility restriction on minimum follow-up (exclusion of studies with <4 weeks) based on expected time needed for motor recovery in the animal model."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

